# =============================================================================
# parent_repo_10_LOC (Level 1) - example environment file
# =============================================================================
#
# This is a TEMPLATE, not a live configuration. It is tracked in version
# control and it documents every environment variable the Level 1 /health
# server reads. There are exactly two of them, and both are listed at the
# bottom of this file with their shipped defaults.
#
# To use it locally, copy it and edit the copy:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a       # export the values into your shell
#     node server.js                 # -> listening on http://0.0.0.0:3000
#     curl -i http://127.0.0.1:3000/health
#
# `.env` is git-ignored (see `.gitignore`) and is never committed. Nothing in
# this repository parses `.env` at run time: no dotenv package is installed,
# because this composition carries zero third-party dependencies and keeps it
# that way. The application reads `process.env` directly, so a variable takes
# effect only once your shell, your container runtime or your CI job has
# exported it. The shortest way to try one is to set it inline for a single
# process:
#
#     PORT=3100 HOST=127.0.0.1 node server.js
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolution precedence - uniform across all three tiers of the composition
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     environment variable  ->  configuration file  ->  compiled-in literal
#          (this file)            config/health.json          (fallback)
#
# Highest precedence first. An environment variable, when it is set to a
# non-empty value, wins over `config/health.json`; `config/health.json` in
# turn wins over the literal defaults compiled into the server.
#
# The literal fallback is required, not optional. It guarantees that /health
# still serves a valid response when `config/health.json` is absent - for
# instance when a container image was built without it. That is precisely the
# failure mode a health endpoint has to survive: an endpoint that cannot answer
# because its own configuration is missing is worse than no endpoint at all,
# because it turns a running application into one that reports itself down.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately NOT overridable here
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * `name` and `version` - the first two members of the /health response body -
#   are NOT environment-overridable at this tier. They are read from
#   `package.json`, this tier's single identity manifest, which declares
#   name `parent_repo_10_LOC` and version `1.0.0`. Do not introduce APP_NAME or
#   APP_VERSION variables: they are not part of the design and nothing reads
#   them.
#
# * The resource path `/health` and the literal `status` value `UP` come from
#   `config/health.json` and are likewise not environment-overridable.
#
# * Levels 2 and 3 of this composition intentionally use their own, differently
#   named variables to override their own listeners, because each of those
#   tiers owns a broader process environment. That asymmetry is deliberate and
#   must not be "harmonized" - do not use their names here, and do not add them
#   to this file. Level 1 uses the plain `PORT` and `HOST` shown below.
#
# * No further variable belongs in this file. Documenting one would advertise a
#   setting that no code reads: this system has no data store, no user sign-in,
#   no transport-layer encryption to terminate and no logging framework. Every
#   value below is a plain, non-sensitive default, and it stays that way -
#   because this file is committed, nothing private may ever be written into
#   it.
#
# The normative definition of the endpoint and of these values is
# `docs/health-endpoint.md`.
# =============================================================================

# Address the /health listener binds to; passed straight through to
# `server.listen()`. Default: 0.0.0.0, meaning every interface, which is what a
# container needs so its published port is reachable from outside. Set it to
# 127.0.0.1 to confine the listener to the loopback interface during local
# development.
#
# Note that 0.0.0.0 is a BIND address only and is never a destination: probes,
# the container HEALTHCHECK and CI all address http://127.0.0.1:<PORT>/health.
HOST=0.0.0.0

# TCP port the /health listener accepts connections on. Default: 3000, this
# tier's reserved port in the composition - Levels 2 and 3 own 8000 and 8080
# respectively, so all three applications can run side by side on one host.
# Change it locally if 3000 is already taken; leave it at 3000 in CI, where the
# workflow probes http://127.0.0.1:3000/health.
PORT=3000
